      "jurisdiction": "Wisconsin state courts",
      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "Kara B. v. Dane County",
          "citation": "205 Wis. 2d 140, 555 N.W.2d 630 (1996)",
          "court": "Wisconsin Supreme Court",
          "year": 1996,
          "date_decided": "1996-11-25",
          "holding": "Foster children have a clearly established Fourteenth Amendment right to safe and secure placement; the conduct of the officials who place and supervise them is measured by the professional judgment standard, and those officials were not entitled to qualified immunity.",
          "relevance": "It gives a judicially enforceable content to the safety outcomes the federal CFSR measures.",
          "quote": "Accordingly, we conclude that the duty of public officials to provide foster children with a safe and secure placement is based on a professional judgment standard.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1721756/kara-b-v-dane-county/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1721756
        },
        {
          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Due Process Clause imposes no affirmative obligation on a county social services agency to protect a child from violence by his father where the child is not in state custody, even though the agency knew of the danger and failed to act.",
          "relevance": "It explains why CFSR monitoring, not litigation, is the operative accountability mechanism for safety failures.",
          "quote": "As a general matter, then, we conclude that a State's failure to protect an individual against private violence simply does not constitute a violation of the Due Process Clause.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Suter v. Artist M.",
          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act's requirement that a state make 'reasonable efforts' to prevent removal and to reunify families confers no privately enforceable right on children or families and creates no implied cause of action.",
          "relevance": "It explains why CFSR review and the Secretary's funding leverage, not litigation, are the operative accountability mechanism for federal performance standards.",
          "quote": "We conclude that 42 U. S. C. § 671 (a)(15) neither confers an enforceable private right on its beneficiaries nor creates an implied cause of action on their behalf.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        }
      ],
